ink this whole  strategy but they were very worried  because they were going to lose a vote  in the house very soon and that first  shutdown they were going to lose control  of their own people and they were going  to be embarrassed in fact the day that  the language was faxed back from the  Capitol giving them a way out of the  shutdown they were sitting around sort  of moping about how are we going to spin  this how are we going to you know once  we lose control of our own party in the  house and they vote to to reopen the  government in some fashion and we have a  choice of whether or not to be taught  what are we going to do then I think  once they saw the poll results of course  that showed them winning two to one and  public opinion on that question of who  caused the shutdown they took a much  decidedly more sanguine view I just just  to follow up on that I just had a  question about how medic Medicare as a  particular program seemed to generate  and there seemed to be a lot of support  among the polling respondents at least  the for that program and yet now oh and  I remember thinking at the time how I  mean there are real problems with  Medicare and keeping it solvent over the  course of several  it's in the future so how was Quentin in  his second term which he now has going  to tackle this and I reading the post  today I think code one of you wrote the  story about others an 18 billion dollar  compromise but but I don't know if there  are any details with regards that in  other words Clinton is hat is having to  grapple with the making reductions in  spending in Medicare right what well I  think they I mean everyone seems to  agree that you have to we have to do  something about Medicare spending the  there's still a big the Republicans and  Democrats still are very far apart on I  think on on Medicare not not only on the  numbers but although the gap is  decreasing but also sort of the policy  how you go about doing it I think the  the administration ear